 "_source": {
  "what": "Measured bird proportions, hand-typed from docs/bird-shape-research.md, which read them off the sources below. Medians only -- facts, not a database -- for the same reason forge/language.py carries hand-typed fish medians: AVONET and the Cornell accounts are not vendored here.",
  "span_over_length and tail_over_length": "docs/bird-shape-research.md section 5. Computed by joining 94 Cornell species accounts (total length, wingspan) to AVONET tail lengths; 88 species joined.",
  "wing_aspect": "docs/bird-shape-research.md section 3. Alerstam, Rosen, Backman, Ericson & Hellgren (2007), 'Flight Speeds among Bird Species', PLoS Biology 5(8):e197, Protocol S1 -- https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.0050197.sd001 -- mass, wingspan and wing area for 138 species from 33,610 individual measurements. AR = b^2/S; 129 species had complete biometry.",
  "THE DENOMINATOR TRAP, RECORDED BEFORE IT BITES": "'Total length' is bill tip to tail tip, so a long tail inflates the denominator and crushes span/length. A magpie measures 1.11 -- the lowest in the set -- purely because its tail is half its total length; on body length alone it is about 2.2. bird.wing_span is authored against total length, so the deflation is consistent; a group median compared against it is comparing like with like ONLY because both carry the same denominator. Do not mix these numbers with a span-over-body-length figure from anywhere else.",
  "groups are assigned BY HAND": "Every species below was placed in a group by hand and the unassigned ones are printed by tools/refshape.py rather than defaulted. The lesson is refs/species-latin.json's: an automated name-to-group step produced a mongoose reference for a lion and passed every quality test this project has.",
  "not sourced here": "Bill depth over bill length by trophic niche exists in docs/bird-shape-research.md section 6 (AVONET, n = 11,009, granivore 0.537 to nectarivore 0.100) and is NOT used, because it needs a hand-assigned diet per species and that assignment was not made. Body depth over length by trophic niche is in the same table and is unused for the same reason."
 },
